From b340e71371d7283e5a058e1e3ea91d1a4b78b254 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <claude@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 23 Aug 2026 08:40:10 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Legacy cleanup: remove the dead synchronous hardware layer

This was already the planned final phase (gated on hardware validation
of the print-flow migration, per the roadmap stated when that migration
shipped) - moved up to run now at explicit request, ahead of outbox/
Hangfire and the Windows Service scripts, since the gating condition
(hardware validation) is now satisfied. Not a duplicate of any other
phase; nothing else in the roadmap touches this layer.

Every deletion below was verified against a repo-wide grep before being
made, not assumed from the controller alone - the risk here was
specifically that some class might be shared between the old
synchronous path and the new async one, which is true for several (see
"kept" list).

Deleted entirely:
- lan/MachineCommands.cs - the whole synchronous HttpWebRequest/ref-
  parameter transport and command-construction class. Confirmed zero
  remaining consumers: MachineController was the last one, and Print's
  earlier migration to IMaticaCommandClient was the last method call
  into it.
- cls/GLOBALS.cs: Machine_Configuration static class (TIPTEMP/PRES/
  CONS/TIME) - its only reader was MachineCommands' old Emboss case,
  which no longer exists; the tipper-parameter phase fully replaced
  this mechanism with real values flowing through PrintReqDto ->
  EmbossRequest, so this wasn't just unused, it was obsolete.
- From lan/CardDataBean.cs (a mixed file - only these nine classes
  removed, not the whole file): CardData, MachineConnectionClass,
  ActionClass, MagData, WriteMAGClass, CoverOpenClass, MoveToChipClass,
  ChipResetClass, ApduExchangeClass. All nine were referenced only by
  the now-deleted MachineCommands.cs and their own declarations -
  confirmed by grep, not assumed. The six protocol-shape classes among
  them (MagData/WriteMAGClass/CoverOpenClass/MoveToChipClass/
  ChipResetClass/ApduExchangeClass) supported commands that were never
  exposed via any endpoint, old or new (CoverOpen/RetractCard/WriteMAG/
  MoveToChip/MoveToCLess) - deleted rather than kept "for later" per
  the explicit instruction to treat this as a complete removal, not a
  partial one. Recoverable from this commit's parent if the deferred
  future phase to expose those commands actually happens.

Deliberately KEPT in lan/CardDataBean.cs, despite living in the same
file as what got deleted - all still real dependencies of the async
layer, not legacy: MachineResponse, MachineConfiguration, MachineInfoJSON
(MachineResponse.MachineStatus is typed as MachineInfoJSON - the class
survives even though the old per-request DI-injected field referencing
it doesn't), LoadCardClass, EjectCardClass, ReadMAGClass, EmbossLine,
EmbossLineClass, AnswerClass, Error (used by MaticaCommandClient for
both command construction and KO-response interpretation).

MachineController: removed the five now-dead fields (ConnectionInfo,
MachineInfo, httpAction, Data, MachineComm) and the now-dead InfoText
field alongside them (zero remaining references once the others were
gone) - constructor simplified to (IPrintFlowClient, IMaticaCommandClient,
Logger). ERROR stays; it's still the out-parameter target for
ENCRYPTION.Enc_TripleDES in Print's local batch write, unrelated to the
deleted layer.

Program.cs: removed the four AddScoped<MachineConnectionClass/
MachineInfoJSON/ActionClass/CardData>() registrations - the controller
constructor was their only consumer. Note this removes DI wiring, not
the MachineInfoJSON/MachineResponse classes themselves, which remain as
plain data models the new async layer deserializes into directly via
JsonConvert rather than via DI.

Not touched: Controllers/HardwareIoPocController.cs (the manually-added
POC controller) - its doc comments mention MachineCommands.cs by name
for context but it has no real dependency on it. Out of scope for this
cleanup since it wasn't part of what was identified as dead, and
touching a file authored outside this patch series wasn't requested;
worth a separate decision on whether to remove it now that its purpose
is served.

No dotnet SDK or NuGet access in the authoring environment. Verified by:
a repo-wide grep for every deleted symbol before deletion (not after -
each class's consumer list was established first, then deleted only
once confirmed to have exactly the expected consumers); a second
repo-wide grep after deletion confirming zero real remaining references
(only doc-comment/string-literal mentions, which are fine as historical
context); the full-repo symbol-table cross-check (four already-known
false positives - properties/enum members literally named Error,
confirmed unchanged from every prior phase, nothing new); brace balance
across every touched file; and JSON/XML validity on appsettings.json,
the .csproj, and both resx files. Please run a full build locally to
confirm - this phase touches shared infrastructure across more files
than any single prior patch, which is exactly the kind of change a
compiler check matters most for.
